**user**:
> I have two directories in a folder for python: `memory` and `tests`. `memory` has an __init__.py. The unit tests in tests/ try to import from memory with "from memory import whatever". But when I call pytest from the root directory, it says No module named 'memory'.
**assistant**:
> This usually happens because Python isn’t seeing your project root on `sys.path`, so `memory` isn’t importable as a top-level package when pytest runs.
> 
> Here are the common fixes, in order of cleanliness:
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Turn the root directory into a package (modern, clean)
> 
> If your structure is like:
> 
> ```text
> project_root/
>     memory/
>         __init__.py
>         ...
>     tests/
>         test_something.py
> ```
> 
> then:
> 
> 1. Add a `pyproject.toml` (or `setup.cfg`) at `project_root/` to define an installable package.
> 2. Give the package a name and src layout if you like.
> 
> Minimal `pyproject.toml` example (PEP 621):
> 
> ```toml
> [build-system]
> requires = ["setuptools>=61.0"]
> build-backend = "setuptools.build_meta"
> 
> [project]
> name = "memory"  # or better, a project name; then set package dir below
> version = "0.0.1"
> ```
> 
> If you prefer a `src/` layout:
> 
> ```text
> project_root/
>     src/
>         memory/
>             __init__.py
>             ...
>     tests/
>         ...
> ```
> 
> and in `pyproject.toml`:
> 
> ```toml
> [tool.setuptools.packages.find]
> where = ["src"]
> ```
> 
> Then install in editable mode:
> 
> ```bash
> cd project_root
> pip install -e .
> pytest
> ```
> 
> Now `from memory import whatever` will work consistently, both in pytest and when running code normally.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Use `PYTHONPATH` for quick-and-dirty local testing
> 
> From the project root:
> 
> ```bash
> PYTHONPATH=. pytest
> ```
> 
> or on Windows:
> 
> ```bat
> set PYTHONPATH=.
> pytest
> ```
> 
> This tells Python to treat the root directory as part of the module search path, making `memory` importable.
> 
> You can also put this in a `pytest.ini`:
> 
> ```ini
> # pytest.ini
> [pytest]
> pythonpath = .
> ```
> 
> Then `pytest` alone should work.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Use relative imports inside `tests/` (not my favorite)
> 
> In your tests, instead of:
> 
> ```python
> from memory import whatever
> ```
> 
> do:
> 
> ```python
> from ..memory import whatever
> ```
> 
> But this only works if `tests` is part of a package hierarchy (i.e. `tests/__init__.py` exists and the parent directory is importable), and it tends to be more brittle. I’d favor 1 or 2.
> 
> ---
> 
> If you tell me:
> 
> - Your exact directory layout (a `tree` output)
> - How you’re invoking pytest (command, working directory)
> - Whether you already have a `pyproject.toml` or `setup.py`
> 
> I can give you a minimal, copy‑paste configuration that will make this work reliably.
